/* Psychology, Social psychology and sociocultural psychiatry. Original cards. */
(window.PA_CARDS = window.PA_CARDS || {}).psych = (window.PA_CARDS.psych || []).concat([
/* ---------- Attitudes and prejudice ---------- */
["Attitudes and prejudice","Structure of attitudes","In the tripartite (ABC) model, what are the three parts of an attitude?","Affective, behavioural and cognitive","An attitude joins feelings about an object, actions or intentions towards it, and beliefs about it."],
["Attitudes and prejudice","Attitude-behaviour link","Which attitudes best predict what a person actually does?","Strong, consistent, specific and easily recalled attitudes","Weak or general attitudes are swamped by the situation; a specific attitude matched to a specific act predicts it far better."],
["Attitudes and prejudice","Attitude-behaviour link","What did LaPiere's 1930s hotel and restaurant study show?","Stated attitudes do not reliably predict behaviour","A Chinese couple was served almost everywhere, yet most of the same places later wrote that they would refuse Chinese guests."],
["Attitudes and prejudice","Measuring attitudes","An attitude questionnaire asks people to rate each statement from 'strongly disagree' to 'strongly agree'. What type of scale is this?","Likert scale","It uses summated ratings: each item is scored, usually 0 to 4 or 1 to 5, and the scores are added."],
["Attitudes and prejudice","Measuring attitudes","What is special about how a Thurstone scale is built?","Judges first give each statement a value on an equal-appearing interval scale","Respondents just agree or disagree; their score is the average (or median) value of the statements they endorse."],
["Attitudes and prejudice","Measuring attitudes","Who devised the semantic differential scale, and what does it use?","Osgood; pairs of opposite adjectives at either end of a 7-point line","For example good-bad or strong-weak; the person marks a point between the two poles."],
["Attitudes and prejudice","Measuring attitudes","Which response bias is the main weakness of the semantic differential?","Positional response bias","People tend to mark the same spot, such as the middle or one end, whatever the adjective pair."],
["Attitudes and prejudice","Cognitive dissonance","Who proposed the theory of cognitive dissonance?","Leon Festinger (1957)","It is a cognitive consistency theory: people are driven to keep their beliefs and actions in line."],
["Attitudes and prejudice","Cognitive dissonance","What is cognitive dissonance?","Uncomfortable tension from holding two inconsistent cognitions, or acting against a belief","It is a product of inconsistency, not of any mental illness."],
["Attitudes and prejudice","Cognitive dissonance","What is the main direct effect of cognitive dissonance on a person?","Psychological discomfort or distress","This unpleasant state is what motivates the person to reduce the inconsistency."],
["Attitudes and prejudice","Cognitive dissonance","What are the three ways people reduce cognitive dissonance?","Change one cognition or behaviour, play down its importance, or add justifying cognitions","Adding a justifying thought is the easiest and most common route because nothing has to be given up."],
["Attitudes and prejudice","Cognitive dissonance","A 52-year-old man who smokes 20 a day says, 'My grandad smoked all his life and lived to 92.' Which dissonance-reducing method is this?","Adding a justifying (consonant) cognition","He keeps smoking and keeps knowing smoking harms, but adds a belief that makes the clash feel smaller."],
["Attitudes and prejudice","Cognitive dissonance","In Festinger and Carlsmith's study, who rated a boring task as more enjoyable: those paid 1 dollar or 20 dollars to praise it?","Those paid 1 dollar","Too little reward to justify lying left dissonance, so they changed their attitude; 20 dollars was justification enough."],
["Attitudes and prejudice","Persuasion","In the Yale model of persuasion, what three groups of factors matter?","The source (communicator), the message and the audience","Hovland's group studied who says what to whom; the channel is sometimes added."],
["Attitudes and prejudice","Persuasion","Which feature of the communicator most increases persuasion?","Credibility: expertise plus trustworthiness","People accept messages from sources they see as knowing the facts and having no reason to mislead."],
["Attitudes and prejudice","Persuasion","What are the two routes in the elaboration likelihood model?","Central route and peripheral route","Central processing weighs argument quality and gives lasting change; peripheral relies on cues like attractiveness and fades."],
["Attitudes and prejudice","Prejudice","In Allport's account of prejudice, what is the behavioural component?","Discrimination","Stereotypes are the cognitive part, dislike the affective part and unfair treatment the behavioural part."],
["Attitudes and prejudice","Prejudice","Under what conditions does intergroup contact reduce prejudice (Allport's contact hypothesis)?","Equal status, shared goals, cooperation and support from authority","Contact alone can reinforce stereotypes; these conditions make it work."],
["Attitudes and prejudice","Prejudice","What is scapegoating?","Displacing frustration onto a weaker, blameless group, such as rising racial hostility in hard economic times","It follows the frustration-aggression idea: the true cause is out of reach, so a safer target is chosen."],
["Attitudes and prejudice","Prejudice","What did Sherif's Robbers Cave study show about intergroup conflict?","Competition for scarce resources breeds hostility, and shared superordinate goals reduce it","This is realistic conflict theory: two boys' groups became hostile when competing and cooperated when they needed each other."],
["Attitudes and prejudice","Prejudice","What does Tajfel's minimal group research show?","Even trivial, random group labels produce in-group favouritism","Social identity theory says we boost self-esteem by valuing our own group over others."],
["Attitudes and prejudice","Prejudice","What is racism?","The belief that ethnic groups differ inherently and some are superior, with resulting discrimination","It combines a false biological idea with unequal treatment and power."],
/* ---------- Attribution and social cognition ---------- */
["Attribution and social cognition","Attribution theory","Who founded attribution theory, and what are its two main types of cause?","Heider; internal (dispositional) and external (situational) causes","People act as naive scientists, explaining behaviour either by the person or by the circumstances."],
["Attribution and social cognition","Attribution theory","What three types of information are used in Kelley's covariation model?","Consensus, distinctiveness and consistency","Do others act the same way? Does the person act this way only here? Do they act this way every time?"],
["Attribution and social cognition","Attribution theory","In Kelley's model, which pattern leads to an internal (personal) attribution?","Low consensus, low distinctiveness and high consistency","Only this person does it, they do it everywhere, and always, so it must be about them."],
["Attribution and social cognition","Attribution biases","What is the fundamental attribution error?","Over-weighting personal disposition and under-weighting the situation when explaining others' behaviour","It is strongest for negative behaviour and in individualistic cultures."],
["Attribution and social cognition","Attribution biases","Observers read essays they knew were written on positions assigned by chance, yet judged writers to hold the views they argued. What bias does this show?","Fundamental attribution error (correspondence bias)","Jones and Harris showed people still infer attitudes even when told the situation forced the behaviour."],
["Attribution and social cognition","Attribution biases","What is the actor-observer bias?","Explaining our own actions by the situation but others' actions by their character","As actors we see the situation; as observers we mainly see the person."],
["Attribution and social cognition","Attribution biases","What is the self-serving bias?","Taking credit for success (internal) and blaming failure on outside factors (external)","It protects self-esteem."],
["Attribution and social cognition","Attribution biases","A 19-year-old male student says his top grade proves he is clever, but his failed paper was due to a bad lecturer and unfair questions. What bias is this?","Self-serving bias","Success is attributed to himself and failure to external causes."],
["Attribution and social cognition","Attribution in psychopathology","In the reformulated learned helplessness model, what attributional style for bad events predicts depression?","Internal, stable and global","'It is my fault, it will last, and it affects everything' (Abramson, Seligman and Teasdale)."],
["Attribution and social cognition","Attribution in psychopathology","Which attributional bias is linked to persecutory delusions?","External attribution for negative events, often to other people","Blaming others for bad events may protect self-esteem but feeds the belief of being persecuted."],
["Attribution and social cognition","Attribution in psychopathology","A 33-year-old man with paranoid schizophrenia on an acute ward says he failed his job interview because his neighbours sabotaged him. Which attributional bias is shown?","Externalising (external-personal) bias for negative events","He blames a negative outcome on the deliberate actions of others."],
["Attribution and social cognition","Person perception","What is the halo effect?","One good (or bad) trait colouring judgements of a person's other traits","First described by Thorndike in ratings of soldiers; it distorts ratings and interviews."],
["Attribution and social cognition","Person perception","A 41-year-old woman with depression sleeps better after a new drug. On a self-rated scale she now rates all her symptoms as much improved, though she is still clearly depressed. What explains this?","Halo effect","One improved feature spreads a favourable glow over her ratings of every other item."],
["Attribution and social cognition","Experimental effects","What is the Hawthorne effect?","Performance improves simply because people know they are being observed","Named after studies at a US factory where output rose with almost any change."],
["Attribution and social cognition","Experimental effects","What is the Pygmalion effect?","Higher expectations from others lead to better performance","Rosenthal and Jacobson found pupils labelled 'bloomers' gained more; it is a self-fulfilling prophecy."],
["Attribution and social cognition","Experimental effects","A 26-year-old female trainee who sat a mock exam scores higher in the real exam than peers who did not. What effect explains this?","Practice effect","Doing a task before improves later performance; it is a key confounder in repeated-measures designs."],
["Attribution and social cognition","Self-concept","What is self-esteem?","How much we value and approve of ourselves","It is the evaluative part of the self-concept, alongside self-image and the ideal self."],
["Attribution and social cognition","Self-concept","What is the first aspect of the self-concept to develop?","The bodily self (sense of one's own body)","Infants first learn where their body ends and the world begins; social and abstract selves come later."],
["Attribution and social cognition","Self-concept","At what age do most children recognise themselves in a mirror (rouge test)?","About 18 months","A child touches a mark on their own nose, not on the mirror image, showing self-recognition."],
["Attribution and social cognition","Theory of mind","What is theory of mind?","The ability to understand that others have beliefs, wishes and intentions different from one's own","It lets us predict and explain behaviour and usually develops by about 4 years."],
["Attribution and social cognition","Theory of mind","The mother of a 7-year-old boy with autism in CAMHS asks why he cannot see things from other children's point of view. What concept explains this?","Impaired theory of mind ('mindblindness')","Baron-Cohen showed autistic children often fail false-belief tasks that most children of the same mental age pass."],
/* ---------- Conformity and obedience ---------- */
["Conformity and obedience","Social influence types","What is the difference between normative and informational social influence?","Normative: going along to be liked or accepted; informational: going along because others seem right","Normative influence often gives public compliance only; informational influence changes private belief."],
["Conformity and obedience","Classic studies","What did Sherif's autokinetic effect study show?","In an ambiguous task, people's estimates converge to form a group norm","A still point of light seems to move in the dark; individuals shifted towards the group estimate (informational influence)."],
["Conformity and obedience","Classic studies","What did Asch's line-judgement study show?","People often conform to a clearly wrong majority","About a third of critical answers conformed and around three quarters of people conformed at least once."],
["Conformity and obedience","Factors affecting conformity","How does group size affect conformity in Asch-type studies?","It rises up to a majority of about three or four, then levels off","Extra people beyond this add little further pressure."],
["Conformity and obedience","Factors affecting conformity","What single change most reduces conformity in a group?","One other person dissenting from the majority","An ally breaks unanimity and conformity falls sharply, even if the ally is also wrong."],
["Conformity and obedience","Factors affecting conformity","How does task ambiguity affect conformity?","Ambiguous or difficult tasks increase conformity","When unsure, people use others as a source of information."],
["Conformity and obedience","Factors affecting conformity","Which personal features make someone more likely to conform?","Low self-confidence, low self-esteem, naivety and being from a collectivist culture","People unsure of their own judgement lean on others; collectivist cultures value group harmony."],
["Conformity and obedience","Factors affecting conformity","Whose views most strongly induce conformity in a group?","High-status members, and members of one's own group","Low-status members are more likely to conform; people conform less to out-group members."],
["Conformity and obedience","Minority influence","According to Moscovici, what makes a minority influential?","Consistency and commitment over time","In the blue-green slides study, a consistent minority changed some majority answers."],
["Conformity and obedience","Deindividuation","What is deindividuation?","Loss of personal identity and accountability when anonymous in a group","It loosens normal restraint and can lead to antinormative acts; the term came from Festinger and was studied by Zimbardo."],
["Conformity and obedience","Deindividuation","A 29-year-old man, usually calm, joins a large masked crowd at a protest and smashes shop windows. What concept best explains this?","Deindividuation","Anonymity in a crowd reduces self-awareness and personal responsibility."],
["Conformity and obedience","Obedience","What did Milgram's experiments (1963) study?","Obedience to authority","Participants were told to give rising electric shocks to a 'learner'; the shocks were fake."],
["Conformity and obedience","Obedience","In Milgram's original study, what proportion went to the maximum 450-volt shock?","About two thirds (65%)","Ordinary people obeyed far more than psychiatrists had predicted."],
["Conformity and obedience","Obedience","Name factors that increased obedience in Milgram's studies.","Experimenter physically present, prestigious setting (Yale), victim out of sight, small step-wise increases","Distance from the victim and closeness to authority made harm easier."],
["Conformity and obedience","Obedience","Name factors that reduced obedience in Milgram's studies.","Victim close by or touched, orders given by phone, disobedient peers, a run-down setting","Proximity to the victim raises empathy; a distant or weak authority loses force."],
["Conformity and obedience","Obedience","What is Milgram's agentic state?","Seeing oneself as an agent carrying out another's wishes, so not responsible","The opposite is the autonomous state, where the person feels responsible for their acts."],
["Conformity and obedience","Obedience","What did the Stanford prison experiment (Zimbardo, 1971) show?","People quickly adopt the behaviour expected of a social role","Student 'guards' became abusive and 'prisoners' passive; it was stopped after 6 days."],
["Conformity and obedience","Compliance techniques","What is the foot-in-the-door technique?","Getting agreement to a small request first makes a larger one more likely","People act consistently with their earlier commitment."],
/* ---------- Groups and leadership ---------- */
["Groups and leadership","Group performance","What is social facilitation?","The presence of others improves simple or well-learned tasks but impairs complex or new ones","Zajonc explained it by arousal, which strengthens the dominant response."],
["Groups and leadership","Group performance","What is social loafing?","Putting in less effort when working in a group than when alone","Ringelmann showed it with tug-of-war: each person pulled less as the team grew."],
["Groups and leadership","Group performance","A 27-year-old female junior doctor works hard on solo projects but contributes little to a team audit of ten people. What explains this?","Social loafing","In a large group she assumes others will carry the work and her own input is not visible."],
["Groups and leadership","Group decisions","What is groupthink?","A drive for agreement in a close group that overrides realistic judgement","Described by Janis; signs include illusion of invulnerability, self-censorship and 'mindguards'."],
["Groups and leadership","Group decisions","How can groupthink be reduced?","Encourage open debate, appoint a devil's advocate, keep the leader impartial and invite outside views","These steps bring dissent and alternatives into the open."],
["Groups and leadership","Group decisions","What is group polarisation?","Group discussion makes the group's view more extreme in the direction it already leaned","The average group decision ends up beyond the average of the members' first views."],
["Groups and leadership","Group decisions","What is the risky shift?","Groups make riskier decisions than individuals would alone","First shown by Stoner; it is a special case of group polarisation."],
["Groups and leadership","Group decisions","Which type of social influence mainly drives group polarisation?","Normative influence (with informational influence also contributing)","Members shift to match and outdo the valued group position; new arguments heard also push views further."],
["Groups and leadership","Group structure","What is a sociogram?","A diagram showing who likes, chooses or relates to whom in a group","Devised by Moreno as part of sociometry; it shows stars, isolates and cliques."],
["Groups and leadership","Group structure","Give Tuckman's stages of group development in order.","Forming, storming, norming, performing, adjourning","Adjourning was added later for groups that disband."],
["Groups and leadership","Group structure","When is a group most likely to tolerate a member breaking its norms?","When the dissenter has high status or skills the group needs","Hollander called this 'idiosyncrasy credit' earned by past contribution."],
["Groups and leadership","Leadership styles","Which three leadership styles did Lewin, Lippitt and White compare?","Autocratic, democratic and laissez-faire","They studied boys' clubs with adult leaders using each style."],
["Groups and leadership","Leadership styles","Which leadership style produced the least work and poorest task completion in Lewin's studies?","Laissez-faire","With no direction, groups were disorganised and got little done."],
["Groups and leadership","Leadership styles","Which leadership style gave the best morale and kept work going when the leader was absent?","Democratic","Members felt ownership of decisions, so motivation was internal."],
["Groups and leadership","Leadership theories","In Fiedler's contingency model, when is a task-oriented leader most effective?","When the situation is either very favourable or very unfavourable for the leader","Relationship-oriented leaders do best in moderately favourable situations."],
["Groups and leadership","Leadership theories","What three factors set how favourable a situation is in Fiedler's model?","Leader-member relations, task structure and position power","Good relations, a clear task and strong formal power make the situation favourable."],
["Groups and leadership","Leadership theories","A 46-year-old female ward manager keeps her team focused on shared goals, makes sure all views are heard and seeks decisions most accept. Which leadership approach is this?","Persuasive (consensus-seeking) leadership","She motivates by persuasion and agreement, not by rewards, punishment or orders."],
["Groups and leadership","Social power","Which base of power comes from the ability to give people what they want?","Reward power","For example bonuses, promotion or time off (French and Raven)."],
["Groups and leadership","Social power","Which base of power comes from the ability to punish?","Coercive power","It gets compliance but often breeds resentment."],
["Groups and leadership","Social power","Which base of power comes from a formal position or role?","Legitimate power","Followers accept that the role-holder has the right to give instructions."],
["Groups and leadership","Social power","Which base of power comes from followers identifying with and admiring the leader?","Referent power","People want to be like the leader and so accept influence."],
["Groups and leadership","Social power","Which base of power comes from special knowledge or skill?","Expert power","Followers defer because they believe the leader knows best, as with a senior clinician."],
/* ---------- Aggression, helping and attraction ---------- */
["Aggression, helping and attraction","Theories of aggression","Who proposed the frustration-aggression hypothesis?","Dollard and colleagues (1939)","In its strong form, frustration always leads to aggression and aggression always comes from frustration."],
["Aggression, helping and attraction","Theories of aggression","Under the frustration-aggression hypothesis, can aggression be directed at someone other than the source of frustration?","Yes; it can be displaced onto a safer, innocent target","This is the basis of scapegoating."],
["Aggression, helping and attraction","Theories of aggression","How did Berkowitz revise the frustration-aggression hypothesis?","Frustration causes anger; aggression needs environmental cues such as weapons","This is the aggressive-cue (weapons effect) theory."],
["Aggression, helping and attraction","Theories of aggression","How did Lorenz explain aggression?","As an innate instinct, controlled in animals by ritual displays and appeasement signals","It is an ethological, biological view."],
["Aggression, helping and attraction","Theories of aggression","What did Bandura's Bobo doll study show?","Children copy aggression they see modelled by adults","This is social learning (observational learning); it was stronger if the model was rewarded."],
["Aggression, helping and attraction","Theories of aggression","A 6-year-old boy brought to CAMHS hits other children in the same way his father hits objects at home. Which learning process best explains this?","Modelling (observational learning)","He learned the behaviour by watching and copying, with no direct reward needed."],
["Aggression, helping and attraction","Types of aggression","How does instrumental aggression differ from hostile aggression?","Instrumental is planned to reach a goal; hostile is impulsive and driven by anger","Kidnap for ransom is instrumental; lashing out in rage is hostile (affective)."],
["Aggression, helping and attraction","Types of aggression","When is aggression highest in groups of baboons?","During periods of social instability, such as changes in rank","Unstable hierarchies raise conflict and stress hormones like cortisol."],
["Aggression, helping and attraction","Helping behaviour","Which 1964 murder in New York led to research on bystander apathy?","Kitty Genovese","Reports that many neighbours did nothing prompted Latané and Darley's studies; the bystander effect is also called Genovese syndrome."],
["Aggression, helping and attraction","Helping behaviour","What is the bystander effect?","People are less likely to help when others are present","Help is more likely from a lone witness (bystander intervention)."],
["Aggression, helping and attraction","Helping behaviour","Give the five steps of Latané and Darley's model of bystander intervention.","Notice the event, see it as an emergency, take responsibility, know how to help, decide to help","Failure at any step means no help is given."],
["Aggression, helping and attraction","Helping behaviour","What is diffusion of responsibility?","Each bystander feels less personally responsible because others could help","'Someone else will do it' grows as the crowd grows."],
["Aggression, helping and attraction","Helping behaviour","What is pluralistic ignorance in an emergency?","Each person looks calm and reads others' calm as a sign nothing is wrong","The group convinces itself there is no emergency."],
["Aggression, helping and attraction","Helping behaviour","A 74-year-old man collapses on a busy station platform. Dozens pass, and none stop. What best explains this?","Bystander apathy (Genovese effect), through diffusion of responsibility","With many people present, each feels less personal responsibility to act."],
["Aggression, helping and attraction","Attraction","Which factor did Newcomb find most important in starting friendships?","Proximity","We are most likely to befriend those we meet often; reciprocity, similarity and complementarity also matter."],
["Aggression, helping and attraction","Attraction","What is the mere exposure effect?","Repeated exposure to something increases liking for it","Described by Zajonc; familiarity breeds liking, part of why proximity works."],
["Aggression, helping and attraction","Relationship theories","Which theory says people choose relationships that give the most rewards for the least cost?","Social exchange theory","Thibaut and Kelley: relationships are judged by their profit compared with other options."],
["Aggression, helping and attraction","Relationship theories","Which theory says relationships are stable when gains and costs feel fair to both partners?","Equity theory","Being over-benefited causes guilt; being under-benefited causes anger."],
["Aggression, helping and attraction","Relationship theories","What are the three parts of Sternberg's triangular theory of love?","Intimacy, passion and commitment","All three together make consummate love."],
["Aggression, helping and attraction","Social motives","Is money a primary or secondary drive?","Secondary (acquired) drive","It has value only because it has been linked to primary needs like food and shelter."],
/* ---------- Sociology of illness and institutions ---------- */
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Founders and critics","Who is called the father of sociology, and what did he study in psychiatry?","Emile Durkheim; suicide","His 1897 book linked suicide rates to social integration and regulation."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Founders and critics","Name Durkheim's four types of suicide.","Egoistic, altruistic, anomic and fatalistic","Too little or too much integration (egoistic, altruistic); too little or too much regulation (anomic, fatalistic)."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Founders and critics","Who wrote 'The Myth of Mental Illness'?","Thomas Szasz (1961)","He argued mental illness is a metaphor for 'problems in living', not true disease."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Founders and critics","Who argued that madness may be a sane response to an insane society?","R. D. Laing","A key figure in anti-psychiatry; he wrote 'The Divided Self'."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Sick role","Who described the sick role?","Talcott Parsons","He saw illness as a form of social deviance with set rights and duties."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Sick role","What are the two rights of the sick role?","Exemption from normal duties, and not being blamed for the illness","These rights are allowed only while the person meets the duties."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Sick role","What are the two duties of the sick role?","Wanting to get well, and seeking and cooperating with competent help","Failing these duties can lead to loss of the rights."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Sick role","A 45-year-old man calls in sick with flu. Which behaviour would Parsons see as outside the sick role: resting, seeing his GP, or blaming himself for the illness?","Blaming himself for the illness","In the sick role the person is not held responsible for being ill."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Sick role","Which behaviour is least consistent with taking the sick role?","Refusing medical help and not accepting being ill","The sick role requires accepting illness and seeking help to recover."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Illness behaviour","Who introduced the term illness behaviour?","David Mechanic","It describes how people notice, interpret and act on symptoms."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Illness behaviour","A 40-year-old man has chest pain and dizziness. His ECG and blood tests are normal. What term covers how he perceives, judges and acts on these symptoms?","Illness behaviour","It is about the response to symptoms, whatever their cause; it varies with culture and personality."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Illness behaviour","What is Engel's biopsychosocial model?","Illness results from biological, psychological and social factors together","Engel (1977) proposed it to widen the narrow biomedical model."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Labelling theory","What is labelling theory of mental illness?","A label can act as a self-fulfilling prophecy, shaping the person's behaviour","Developed by Lemert and Scheff; deviance lies in how others name behaviour."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Labelling theory","A 24-year-old man seen as a bit suspicious at work is admitted with schizophrenia. Afterwards colleagues avoid him and he becomes much more suspicious. Which concept fits his later suspiciousness?","Secondary deviance","Behaviour that follows from being labelled and treated as mentally ill."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Labelling theory","A 50-year-old man on a coach laughs and argues loudly with himself. Other passengers find it odd but cannot name a broken rule. What is Scheff's term for this?","Residual rule-breaking","Breaking unwritten rules of normal conduct that have no specific label; it may then be labelled mental illness."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Institutions","Who wrote 'Asylums' (1961) and described the total institution?","Erving Goffman","He studied a large US psychiatric hospital as a participant observer."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Institutions","What did Goffman mean by mortification of the self?","Stripping away a new inmate's former identity","Removal of clothes, possessions and roles (role stripping) makes the person an 'inmate'."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Institutions","What was Goffman's 'betrayal funnel'?","Family and friends report the patient's behaviour to professionals, leading to admission","It is the first step of the patient's 'moral career' into the institution."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Institutions","Who described institutional neurosis?","Russell Barton (1959)","Signs include apathy, loss of initiative, submissiveness and a typical stooped posture."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Institutions","A 63-year-old man discharged after 30 years in a long-stay hospital cannot cook or manage a flat on his own. What is this called?","Institutionalisation","Long institutional life removes daily skills and initiative; rehabilitation must rebuild them."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Institutions","What did Wing and Brown's 'Three Hospitals' study show?","Poor, understimulating ward environments were linked to more negative symptoms","Improving the social setting reduced this clinical poverty syndrome."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Institutions","A 35-year-old woman on an acute ward is allowed daily leave if she gives a urine drug screen on return. What sociological term describes this kind of agreed ward rule?","Negotiated order","Strauss described how hospital rules are bargained between staff and patients."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Social class","What does the social drift hypothesis say about schizophrenia and class?","Illness causes people to drift down the social scale","Goldberg and Morrison found patients' fathers had a normal class spread while patients had fallen."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Social class","In which disorder are the brothers and children of patients over-represented in higher social classes?","Bipolar affective disorder","Families of origin of people with bipolar disorder tend to come from higher social classes."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Social class","In clinical samples, which eating disorder is more common in higher social classes?","Anorexia nervosa","This older finding may partly reflect who reaches services."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Social class","What is the Jarman index?","A UK score of area deprivation (underprivileged area score) built from census data","Brian Jarman used items such as elderly people living alone, lone parents and unemployment."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Health behaviour","Name the stages of change in Prochaska and DiClemente's model.","Precontemplation, contemplation, preparation, action, maintenance (and relapse)","People cycle through stages; help should fit the current stage."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Health behaviour","A 50-year-old man says his drinking is not a problem and he has no plan to cut down. Which stage of change is he in?","Precontemplation","He does not see a problem, so motivational work should raise awareness."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Health behaviour","A 40-year-old man has stayed off alcohol since inpatient detox ended. In the stages of change model, which stage is he in?","Action (maintenance begins after about 6 months)","Under 6 months of change is action; beyond that is maintenance."],
["Sociology of illness and institutions","Health behaviour","Which population policy has the best evidence for reducing alcohol harm?","Raising the price of alcohol (for example minimum unit pricing)","Price affects how much is drunk, especially by heavy drinkers; Scotland brought in minimum unit pricing in 2018."],
/* ---------- Stigma ---------- */
["Stigma","Concepts","Who wrote 'Stigma: Notes on the Management of Spoiled Identity'?","Erving Goffman (1963)","He defined stigma as an attribute that is deeply discrediting."],
["Stigma","Concepts","What three types of stigma did Goffman describe?","Abominations of the body, blemishes of character, and tribal stigma","Physical deformity; perceived moral flaws such as mental illness; and race, nation or religion."],
["Stigma","Concepts","According to Thornicroft, what three problems make up stigma?","Ignorance (knowledge), prejudice (attitudes) and discrimination (behaviour)","Each needs a different response: education, attitude change and legal protection."],
["Stigma","Types of stigma","What is enacted stigma?","Actual discrimination experienced by a person","Scambler and Hopkins contrasted it with felt stigma."],
["Stigma","Types of stigma","What is felt stigma?","Shame and fear of being discriminated against","It can be more disabling than enacted stigma, as people hide and avoid help."],
["Stigma","Types of stigma","A 43-year-old woman returning to work after depression is asked at interview to give detailed accounts of her detention under the Mental Health Act. What type of stigma is this?","Enacted stigma","She is being treated unfairly in practice, not just fearing it."],
["Stigma","Types of stigma","A 12-year-old girl is called names at school after her father is admitted to the local psychiatric unit. What is this called?","Courtesy stigma (associative or family stigma)","Stigma spreads to people linked to the stigmatised person."],
["Stigma","Types of stigma","What is self-stigma?","Accepting public stereotypes and applying them to oneself","It lowers self-esteem and leads to the 'why try' effect, reducing help-seeking."],
["Stigma","Types of stigma","A 38-year-old male nurse says no person with mental illness can make their own decisions. Which thinking error is this?","Overgeneralisation (stereotyping)","A feature of some people is wrongly applied to the whole group."],
["Stigma","Drivers of stigma","Which features of an illness make stigma more likely?","Perceived dangerousness, unpredictability, personal blame and disruption of social interaction","Visible conditions that disturb social exchange are most stigmatised."],
["Stigma","Drivers of stigma","What are the main recurring themes behind stigma towards mental illness?","Dangerousness, unpredictability, being to blame and poor outlook","Cost of treatment is not a usual theme."],
["Stigma","Drivers of stigma","What does NIMBY mean in mental health?","'Not in my back yard': local opposition to mental health services nearby","It reflects fear of danger and lower property values."],
["Stigma","Reducing stigma","Which intervention has the best evidence for reducing stigma?","Social contact with people who have lived experience of mental illness","Contact works better than education alone, especially when it is personal and equal."],
["Stigma","Reducing stigma","Which major anti-stigma campaign ran in England from 2007 to 2021?","Time to Change","Led by Mind and Rethink Mental Illness; one of the longest running anti-stigma campaigns."],
["Stigma","Reducing stigma","What was the Royal College of Psychiatrists' 1998 to 2003 anti-stigma campaign?","Changing Minds","It targeted public and professional stigma; it is separate from the Care Programme Approach."],
["Stigma","Reducing stigma","Under the Equality Act 2010, when is a mental illness a disability?","When it has a substantial, long-term (12 months or more) adverse effect on normal daily activities","Employers must then make reasonable adjustments and must not discriminate."],
/* ---------- Life events and the family ---------- */
["Life events and the family","Life events","Which scale did Holmes and Rahe publish in 1967?","The Social Readjustment Rating Scale","It lists 43 life events, each with a life change unit score."],
["Life events and the family","Life events","On the Holmes and Rahe scale, which three events score highest?","Death of a spouse (100), divorce (73), marital separation (65)","Marriage was the anchor, set at 50."],
["Life events and the family","Life events","Which interview rates the threat of life events in the light of each person's social context?","The Life Events and Difficulties Schedule (LEDS)","Brown and Harris developed it; trained raters judge 'contextual threat'."],
["Life events and the family","Life events","According to Kendler (2003), which type of life event predicts pure major depression?","Humiliation events","Danger events predicted generalised anxiety, while loss plus danger predicted mixed states."],
["Life events and the family","Life events","A 48-year-old woman becomes depressed after losing her job of 15 years. Her new job ends within two weeks and she feels stuck with no way out. Which life event dimension fits?","Entrapment","The usual urge to escape stress is blocked, which links to depression and suicidality."],
["Life events and the family","Life events","Are recurrent depressive episodes preceded by more or fewer life events than first episodes?","Fewer","Post's kindling theory says episodes become more autonomous with each recurrence."],
["Life events and the family","Life events","Why may a depressed person report more adverse life events than controls?","Recall bias: low mood makes negative events easier to recall (effort after meaning)","Positive events are similar in depressed and non-depressed people."],
["Life events and the family","Life events","Which gene variant interacted with life events to predict later depression in Caspi's 2003 study?","Short allele of the serotonin transporter promoter (5-HTTLPR)","Carriers had more depression after stress; later replications have been mixed."],
["Life events and the family","Life events","In schizophrenia, when do life events tend to cluster before relapse (Brown and Birley)?","In the 3 weeks before relapse","This supports the stress-vulnerability model."],
["Life events and the family","Brown and Harris","What did Brown and Harris study in 'Social Origins of Depression' (1978)?","Depression in women in Camberwell, south London","They described provoking agents, vulnerability factors and symptom formation factors."],
["Life events and the family","Brown and Harris","Name Brown and Harris's four vulnerability factors for depression in women.","Loss of mother before 11, no confiding relationship, three or more children under 14 at home, no paid work outside home","These raise the risk of depression only when a provoking event occurs."],
["Life events and the family","Brown and Harris","Which Brown and Harris vulnerability factor has had the most support since?","Lack of a confiding relationship","A close, trusted relationship protects against depression after severe events."],
["Life events and the family","Expressed emotion","Which three parts of expressed emotion best predict relapse?","Critical comments, hostility and emotional over-involvement","Warmth and positive remarks are also rated but are not part of high EE."],
["Life events and the family","Expressed emotion","Which tool is the gold standard for measuring expressed emotion?","The Camberwell Family Interview","A semi-structured interview with the relative, recorded and rated later by trained staff."],
["Life events and the family","Expressed emotion","Roughly how do 9-month relapse rates compare for people with schizophrenia in high and low EE homes?","About 50% with high EE versus about 20% with low EE","Medication and less face-to-face contact lower relapse in high EE homes."],
["Life events and the family","Expressed emotion","With a high EE relative, what amount of face-to-face contact raises relapse risk?","More than 35 hours a week","Less contact (for example day services) is protective; in low EE homes more contact may help."],
["Life events and the family","Expressed emotion","A 32-year-old man with schizophrenia improves on an acute ward but worsens after each home leave. His mother calls him lazy and useless. What should the team offer next?","Family intervention","High critical EE is driving relapse; family work lowers EE and relapse."],
["Life events and the family","Expressed emotion","What does NICE recommend for families of people with psychosis who live with or are in close contact with them?","Family intervention, at least 10 planned sessions over 3 months to 1 year","It should include the person with psychosis where possible and reduces relapse and admission."],
["Life events and the family","Family theories","Who coined the term schizophrenogenic mother?","Frieda Fromm-Reichmann (1948)","The idea that a cold, controlling mother causes schizophrenia is now rejected."],
["Life events and the family","Family theories","What is Bateson's double bind?","A child gets conflicting messages and cannot escape or comment on the conflict","Bateson (1956) proposed it as a cause of schizophrenia; it is not supported."],
["Life events and the family","Family theories","Which family pattern did Wynne describe, in which a false show of closeness hides conflict?","Pseudomutuality","Wynne also described the 'rubber fence' around such families."],
["Life events and the family","Family theories","With which condition is the discredited term 'refrigerator mother' linked?","Autism","Kanner and later Bettelheim blamed cold parenting; autism is now known to be mainly genetic and neurodevelopmental."],
["Life events and the family","Parental illness","A 14-year-old boy's mother has recurrent depression. Which disorder is he most at risk of before age 20?","Depression","Children of depressed parents have about 2 to 3 times the risk, through genes and environment."],
["Life events and the family","Child maltreatment","Are adults who were physically abused as children more likely to abuse their own children?","Yes, though most do not","Around a third go on to maltreat their children; this is intergenerational transmission."],
/* ---------- Culture, ethnicity and migration ---------- */
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Key terms","What best defines a person's ethnicity?","Group identification based on shared ancestry, culture, language or religion","It is largely self-defined, unlike older ideas of race based on physical features."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Key terms","What is enculturation?","Learning one's own culture from family, peers, school and media","It happens to everyone, whether or not they migrate."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Key terms","What is an explanatory model of illness (Kleinman)?","The patient's own view of what the illness is, its cause and its cure","Asking about it builds trust and improves engagement."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Berry's acculturation model","In Berry's model, what is integration?","Keeping one's own culture while taking part in the new one","It is linked to the best mental health outcomes."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Berry's acculturation model","In Berry's model, what is assimilation?","Taking on the host culture and giving up most of one's original culture","The person seeks contact with the host culture but does not keep their own identity."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Berry's acculturation model","In Berry's model, what is marginalisation?","Losing one's own culture without joining the host culture","It carries the highest risk of distress and mental illness."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Berry's acculturation model","A 45-year-old male Sri Lankan refugee in the UK wants to keep his cultural identity and also mix with British people. Which acculturation strategy is this?","Integration","He holds on to his culture and takes part in the new one."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Berry's acculturation model","A 33-year-old South African woman in London lets go of her own cultural values but does not engage with the new culture either. Which strategy is this?","Marginalisation","She belongs to neither culture."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Migration and adaptation","A 39-year-old male Asian migrant practises his religion and customs at home, works well in English and is fluent in both languages. What is this called?","Biculturalism","He moves comfortably between two cultures; it is a form of integration."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Migration and adaptation","What does the melting pot model describe?","Many cultures blending into one new shared culture","Most used of the United States; multiculturalism ('salad bowl') keeps cultures distinct."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Migration and adaptation","In a migrating family, which members usually find it hardest to adapt to the new culture?","The grandparents (older migrants)","Older people have more settled identities and fewer chances to learn the language; children adapt fastest."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Migration and psychosis","Which factor is most strongly linked to a raised risk of schizophrenia in a young man?","Being a migrant (or child of migrants)","Risk is raised in first and second generations and is highest in Black African and Caribbean groups in the UK."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Migration and psychosis","A 20-year-old Jamaican man living in the UK develops schizophrenia. How does the rate of schizophrenia in his parents compare with that of parents of white British patients?","It is similar","Rates are not raised in Jamaica itself, so genetic differences do not explain the excess in migrants."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Migration and psychosis","What is now thought to explain the higher rate of schizophrenia in UK migrant groups?","Social adversity, such as discrimination, isolation and disadvantage","Selective migration and misdiagnosis do not explain it."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Migration and psychosis","How do detention rates under the Mental Health Act compare for Black people in England?","They are detained over three times more often than white people","This persistent gap drove the 2018 Independent Review of the Mental Health Act."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Culture and psychopathology","In Tseng's model, what is a pathoplastic effect of culture?","Culture shapes the content and form of symptoms","For example, delusions about witchcraft or technology depending on setting."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Culture and psychopathology","A 30-year-old Malaysian man, shamed in public, takes a weapon and attacks people at random. In Tseng's model, what effect of culture is this?","Pathoselective","Culture shapes which way a person chooses to react to stress; this is amok."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Culture and psychopathology","A 23-year-old woman goes into brief startle-induced dissociative states and is teased into them at social events for entertainment. Which effect of culture is this?","Pathoelaborating","Culture reinforces and elaborates the behaviour; this pattern is latah."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Culture and psychopathology","Cultural acceptance of heavy drinking raises the rate of alcohol problems. In Tseng's model, what effect is this?","Pathofacilitating","Culture does not change the disorder's form but makes it more common."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Cultural concepts of distress","What is koro?","Intense fear that the penis (or breasts or vulva) is shrinking into the body and will cause death","Seen mainly in South and East Asia, sometimes in epidemics."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Cultural concepts of distress","What is amok?","A brooding period followed by sudden, violent attacks on others, then amnesia and exhaustion","First described in Malaysia, mainly in men."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Cultural concepts of distress","What is latah?","Startle-induced dissociation with echolalia, echopraxia and obedience","Mostly seen in middle-aged women in Malaysia and Indonesia."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Cultural concepts of distress","What is dhat syndrome?","Anxiety and fatigue blamed on loss of semen in urine","Seen in young men in South Asia; it often overlaps with depression or anxiety."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Cultural concepts of distress","A 22-year-old male West African student has poor concentration, poor sleep, anxiety and a feeling of pressure in his head. Which culture-bound syndrome is this?","Brain fag","Linked to 'thinking too much' from study; it overlaps with anxiety and depression."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Cultural concepts of distress","A 31-year-old Inuit woman becomes suddenly excited, tears off her clothes and runs into icy water. What is the likely culture-bound syndrome?","Piblokto (Arctic hysteria)","A brief dissociative episode, often followed by sleep and amnesia."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Cultural concepts of distress","A 42-year-old Chinese man will not leave home because he fears cold wind will drain his vital energy and kill him. What is this?","Pa-leng (frigophobia)","A morbid fear of cold, tied to yin-yang beliefs about losing vital heat."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Cultural concepts of distress","What is taijin kyofusho?","Fear that one's body or behaviour offends or embarrasses others","A Japanese form of social anxiety focused on others' discomfort, not one's own."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Cultural concepts of distress","A 27-year-old Japanese man avoids friends because he believes he gives off an offensive body odour, despite reassurance. What is this?","Jikoshu-kyofu","A form of taijin kyofusho centred on body odour; similar to olfactory reference disorder."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Cultural concepts of distress","What is hwa-byung?","Korean 'anger illness' from suppressed anger, with a feeling of a mass in the chest","Often seen in middle-aged women with long-standing family conflict."],
["Culture, ethnicity and migration","Cultural practices","According to the WHO, roughly how many girls and women alive today have undergone female genital mutilation?","More than 200 million","It is illegal in the UK; health workers in England and Wales must report known FGM in girls under 18 to the police."],
/* ---------- Medical and research ethics ---------- */
["Medical and research ethics","Ethical theories","What is utilitarianism?","The right action is the one that brings the greatest good for the greatest number","A consequentialist theory linked to Bentham and Mill."],
["Medical and research ethics","Ethical theories","What is the difference between consequentialist and deontological ethics?","Consequentialist judges acts by outcomes; deontological judges them by duty and rules","Kant is the classic deontologist: some acts are wrong whatever the result."],
["Medical and research ethics","Ethical theories","A 35-year-old man with paranoid schizophrenia kills a shopkeeper. Despite advice that he needs treatment, the court sends him to prison to protect the public. Which ethical approach fits?","Utilitarianism","Public safety (the good of many) is put ahead of the needs of one person."],
["Medical and research ethics","Four principles","Who proposed the four prima facie principles of medical ethics?","Beauchamp and Childress","Respect for autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence and justice (1979)."],
["Medical and research ethics","Four principles","A 58-year-old woman had kidney damage on lithium. Her psychiatrist decides not to restart it. Which principle is this?","Non-maleficence","Avoiding a known risk of serious harm."],
["Medical and research ethics","Four principles","A 76-year-old man with Parkinson's disease dementia is offered a choice of rivastigmine tablets or patches. Which principle is this?","Respect for autonomy","He is helped to make his own decision about his care."],
["Medical and research ethics","Four principles","A mental health service manager must cut either a day hospital or an early intervention team after a budget cut. Which principle is most at stake?","Justice (distributive justice)","It is about fair sharing of limited resources."],
["Medical and research ethics","Four principles","What is medical paternalism?","A doctor acting on what they think is best, overriding or ignoring the patient's wishes","It conflicts with autonomy; withholding a likely diagnosis is an example."],
["Medical and research ethics","Four principles","A 62-year-old woman's routine bone scan shows a likely tumour. Her doctors do not tell her and just repeat scans. What approach is this?","Paternalism","Information is withheld for her supposed good, denying her an informed choice."],
["Medical and research ethics","Historical codes","Which ancient Indian physician's code stressed compassion, confidentiality and continued learning?","Charaka","His code also asked physicians to be devoted to the patient's good."],
["Medical and research ethics","Research ethics","What is the first and most important principle of the Nuremberg Code (1947)?","Voluntary consent of the human subject is essential","It followed the trial of Nazi doctors for experiments on prisoners."],
["Medical and research ethics","Research ethics","What gave rise to the Nuremberg Code?","The Doctors' Trial of Nazi physicians after the Second World War","It tried German doctors, not Japanese doctors."],
["Medical and research ethics","Research ethics","What guides medical research on human participants worldwide today?","The Declaration of Helsinki","Adopted by the World Medical Association in 1964 and revised regularly, most recently in 2024."],
["Medical and research ethics","Research ethics","What three principles did the US Belmont Report set out for research?","Respect for persons, beneficence and justice","Respect for persons covers autonomy and consent."],
["Medical and research ethics","Research ethics","In which study were children with learning disabilities deliberately infected with hepatitis?","The Willowbrook study","Run at a New York state school from the 1950s to 1970s; parents were pressured to consent."],
["Medical and research ethics","Research ethics","Once a research participant has signed a consent form, must they finish the study?","No; they can withdraw at any time without giving a reason","Consent is an ongoing process, not a contract."],
["Medical and research ethics","Research ethics","Can research participants be paid or recruited by advertising?","Yes, if approved by an ethics committee and payment is not so large it is coercive","Payment must not be an undue inducement to accept risk."],
["Medical and research ethics","Professional guidance","Which body publishes 'Good Medical Practice' for UK doctors?","The General Medical Council (GMC)","It sets the standards and values expected of all registered doctors."],
["Medical and research ethics","Capacity and consent","What are the four parts of the capacity test under the Mental Capacity Act 2005?","Understand, retain and use or weigh the information, and communicate a decision","Failing any one because of an impairment of mind or brain means lack of capacity for that decision."],
["Medical and research ethics","Capacity and consent","A 30-year-old woman in A&E after an overdose refuses a nasogastric tube. She has capacity. What should happen?","Respect her refusal and do not insert the tube","A capacitous adult may refuse even life-saving treatment; keep offering it and keep reviewing capacity."],
["Medical and research ethics","Law and liability","Are torts dealt with in civil or criminal law?","Civil law","Torts include negligence, defamation, trespass and nuisance; the remedy is usually damages."],
["Medical and research ethics","Law and liability","Which class of psychotropic medication is most likely to impair driving?","Benzodiazepines","They slow reaction time and reduce alertness, especially early on or after a dose rise."],
]);
